# ============================================================================
# gromacs.def — Singularity/Apptainer definition for a GPU-enabled GROMACS
# build, portable across the project's TWO clusters (banyan H100 + dgx1 V100).
#
# STATUS: this FILE (Route A) has never itself been built — `singularity build
#         gromacs.sif gromacs.def` was attempted attended on banyan 2026-07-29
#         and failed in seconds (no /etc/subuid mapping; see ./README.md's
#         fakeroot section for the captured FATAL error). ITS DOCKER TWIN HAS
#         BEEN BUILT: `docker build` from ./Dockerfile (the SAME pins and
#         %post steps, translated) succeeded the same day — Slurm job 30, exit
#         0, `gromacs-p53mdm2:latest` (`70659e395c53`, 10.6 GB). No `.sif`
#         exists yet either way; converting the Docker image and putting a
#         `.sif` on shared home is still a PI-GATED mutating step (see
#         ./README.md and __reports__/p53-mdm2/13-route_b_build_observed_v0.md).
#
# WHY SINGULARITY (not "docker run"): see __reports__/p53-mdm2/07-cluster_liveverify_v1.md.
#   Singularity is the ONLY portable, non-root, Slurm-integrated run path across
#   BOTH clusters. Docker works interactively on banyan (user in docker group)
#   but does NOT exist for this user on dgx1, and a bare `docker run` bypasses
#   Slurm GPU isolation. submit_job wraps `singularity exec --nv` on GPU jobs.
#
# HOW TO BUILD (both PI-gated — do NOT run unattended):
#   Route B (SUCCEEDED 2026-07-29 — Docker first, then convert): on banyan,
#       which has the Docker daemon 29.4.3 with this user in the `docker` group:
#         docker build -t gromacs-p53mdm2 .    # SAME base + %post steps
#         docker save gromacs-p53mdm2 -o gromacs.tar
#         singularity build gromacs.sif docker-archive://gromacs.tar
#       The daemon builds as root, so %post's apt-get + compile just work.
#       The FIRST of these three commands has run and succeeded (Slurm job 30,
#       exit 0, 5m27s, `gromacs-p53mdm2:latest` id `70659e395c53`, 10.6 GB); the
#       `docker save` + `singularity build docker-archive://` conversion has NOT
#       yet run — that is `sif_delivery/convert_verify_cleanup`, a separate leaf.
#         [source: __reports__/p53-mdm2/13-route_b_build_observed_v0.md §1-2]
#   Route A (DEAD BY OBSERVATION): native `singularity build gromacs.sif
#       gromacs.def` on banyan. banyan does have singularity-ce 4.2.2, BUT this
#       user has NO /etc/subuid + /etc/subgid mapping on EITHER cluster (banyan
#       lists only `user` and `test`; dgx1 only `lxd` and `root`), and the %post
#       below needs root-in-container, i.e. --fakeroot, which needs that mapping.
#       Unprivileged userns is otherwise enabled on banyan
#       (kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone = 1), so the blocker is specifically the
#       missing subuid range, not a kernel lockdown.
#         [source: __reports__/p53-mdm2/10-cluster_state_refresh_v0.md §3
#                  (read-only live refresh, cycle-006, commit c8f2cc2)]
#       CONFIRMED 2026-07-30 — this is now an OBSERVED failure, not inference.
#       The attended command was run: `singularity build gromacs.sif gromacs.def`
#       failed in seconds with `FATAL: --remote, --fakeroot, or the proot
#       command are required to build this source as a non-root user` (exit
#       255). `proot` is confirmed absent from banyan's PATH, and `--remote` is
#       declined on principle (ships the recipe to a third-party cloud
#       builder). All three of the error's named alternatives are closed.
#         [source: __reports__/p53-mdm2/13-route_b_build_observed_v0.md §7-8]
#       (Report 07's earlier claim that banyan "has fakeroot" was a
#        capability-vs-entitlement conflation: the RUNTIME supports fakeroot; this
#        USER is not entitled to it. That claim is hereby retracted.)
#   Podman is a LEAD, not a route: banyan has podman 3.4.4 reporting
#       `Rootless: true` [same source §3], but rootless podman builds normally
#       need subuid ranges too, and this was never tested.
#   Because home is one shared NFS (ts2:/export/home), a .sif built/staged once
#   is visible from both clusters — stage-once, run-anywhere (this specific claim
#   is itself still untested pending a real .sif; see `sif_delivery`).
#   Build scratch: keep it OFF banyan's root filesystem. `/tmp` is on `/`, which
#   fell 586 G -> 439 G free between 2026-07-21 and 2026-07-27 [same source §5]
#   but then HELD at 439 G free across four further days, and the 2026-07-29
#   build itself only cost ~9 G net — a one-off event, not an ongoing drain,
#   though its original cause is still uninvestigated
#   [source: __reports__/p53-mdm2/13-route_b_build_observed_v0.md §6]. Export
#   SINGULARITY_TMPDIR / SINGULARITY_CACHEDIR / TMPDIR under the shared home and
#   re-check `df -h /` immediately before building. See ./README.md gated step 1.
#
# VERSION-PINNING PROVENANCE (cycle-006 upstream verification pass):
#   Every pinned value below now carries the source URL it was checked against.
#   NO `[assumption]` items remain. The last one — GMX_SIMD — was resolved later
#   in cycle-006 once the cluster CPU models were finally recorded by the live
#   refresh; see the [verified] note next to the flag.
# ============================================================================

Bootstrap: docker
# --- Base image: CUDA 12.9.1 devel on Ubuntu 22.04 ---------------------------
# CUDA 12.9 is deliberate, not arbitrary. This is the CROSS-CLUSTER portability
# pivot of the whole recipe:
#   * dgx1  = 8x Tesla V100-SXM2-16GB  -> compute capability 7.0 (Volta, sm_70)
#             driver 580.159.03  [live: report 07]
#   * banyan= 2x H100 NVL ~94GB        -> compute capability 9.0 (Hopper, sm_90)
#             driver 595.71.05   [live: report 07]
#
# NVIDIA CUDA 13.0 REMOVES offline compilation for compute capability < 7.5,
# which includes Volta (V100). NVIDIA's own guidance, verbatim: "If you're
# writing applications that support GPUs of CC earlier than 7.5, then you'll
# want to install and remain on the following: NVIDIA Driver: Remain on branch
# 580 [...] CUDA Toolkit: Remain on 12.9."
#   [verified 2026-07-27: https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/navigating-gpu-architecture-support-a-guide-for-nvidia-cuda-developers/
#    (NVIDIA developer blog, published 2025-08-04); branch 580 is stated there
#    to be an LTS driver supported until mid-2028.]
# Independently CONFIRMED against the toolkits' own nvcc manuals — the removal
# is not merely announced, it shipped:
#   * CUDA 12.9.1 nvcc "Streaming Multiprocessor (SM) Architectures" list
#     CONTAINS both sm_70 and sm_90
#     [verified: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/archive/12.9.1/cuda-compiler-driver-nvcc/index.html]
#   * CUDA 13.0.0 nvcc's same list STARTS AT sm_75 — sm_70 is absent
#     [verified: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/archive/13.0.0/cuda-compiler-driver-nvcc/index.html]
# dgx1 is on driver branch 580 EXACTLY, so CUDA 12.9 is the last toolkit that
# compiles first-class sm_70 kernels for it. => 12.9 is the single toolkit
# covering BOTH clusters (sm_70 + sm_90).
#
# Runtime note (Singularity --nv): the CONTAINER ships the CUDA runtime; the
# HOST driver is bind-mounted in by `--nv`. A newer host driver running an older
# CUDA runtime is always forward-compatible, so 12.9 runs fine under host
# drivers 580 (dgx1) and 595 (banyan).
#
# TAG EXISTS — no longer an assumption. `nvidia/cuda:12.9.1-devel-ubuntu22.04`
# is published on Docker Hub, last updated 2025-07-25, manifest digest
#   sha256:bd4e2680a261c212f1e2fea241606f71497dc67a417f73175d794ec8212b5ba8
# (amd64 image ~5.67 GB compressed-manifest size)
#   [verified 2026-07-27: https://hub.docker.com/v2/repositories/nvidia/cuda/tags/12.9.1-devel-ubuntu22.04].
# A newer 12.9 patch tag `12.9.2-devel-ubuntu22.04` also exists (updated
# 2026-05-20) — bump deliberately if wanted, but 12.9.1 is what EasyBuild's own
# GROMACS 2025.3 CUDA easyconfig targets (see GROMACS section below), so 12.9.1
# is the better-corroborated combination. To pin immutably, replace the tag with
# `nvidia/cuda@sha256:bd4e26...` (digest above).
#
# WHY ubuntu22.04 AND NOT ubuntu24.04 (this is a compiler decision, not inertia):
#   * jammy's `build-essential` gives gcc 11.x (apt `gcc` = 4:11.2.0-1ubuntu1)
#     [verified: https://packages.ubuntu.com/jammy/gcc]. GROMACS 2025.3 says
#     verbatim: "We recommend to use the GCC compiler, version 9.x to 11.x.
#     Note: there are known issues with GCC 12 and newer."
#     [verified: https://manual.gromacs.org/documentation/2025.3/install-guide/index.html]
#   * noble ships gcc 13.2 [verified: https://packages.ubuntu.com/noble/gcc],
#     i.e. squarely inside GROMACS' "known issues" range.
#   * Ubuntu 22.04 LTS is a natively supported distro for CUDA 12.9
#     [verified: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/archive/12.9.1/cuda-installation-guide-linux/index.html].
# COST of that choice, handled in %post: jammy's apt `cmake` is 3.22.1
# [verified: https://packages.ubuntu.com/jammy/cmake] but GROMACS 2025.3
# "builds with the CMake build system, requiring at least version 3.28"
# [verified: install guide, same URL as above]. So CMake must NOT come from apt
# here — a pinned upstream CMake is installed below. (noble's cmake 3.28.3 would
# satisfy that, but the gcc-13 problem outweighs the convenience.)
#
# `-devel` (not `-runtime`) is required: it carries nvcc + headers to COMPILE
# GROMACS. Slimming to a multi-stage -runtime image is a later optimization.
From: nvidia/cuda:12.9.1-devel-ubuntu22.04

# NOTE: no `BuildStatus` key here (removed 2026-07-30). A %labels entry is
# baked into the image and carried into the .sif, so a literal value here
# would become a PERMANENT, wrong provenance claim — `singularity inspect`
# would forever report the not-yet-built status this key used to carry, even
# after a real build (as this one now is via the Docker twin; see
# ./README.md). Build status is per-build state, not a recipe constant. If
# per-build status is ever wanted, the right mechanism is a build-time label
# argument, not a literal baked in here.
%labels
    Maintainer   usd-bio p53-mdm2 track
    Purpose      GPU-enabled GROMACS for p53-MDM2 self-run MD (p1b Step 2)
    GromacsVer   2025.3
    CudaToolkit  12.9.1
    CMakeVer     4.0.3
    TargetSM     70;90   # V100 (dgx1) + H100 (banyan)

%post
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Pinned versions. Bump deliberately, never float `latest`.
    # -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    set -eu
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

    # --- GROMACS source pin --------------------------------------------------
    # 2025.3 exists and its docs/download page is live; the tarball is present in
    # the ftp index (gromacs-2025.3.tar.gz, 42 MB, dated 2025-08-29)
    #   [verified: https://manual.gromacs.org/documentation/2025.3/download.html
    #              https://ftp.gromacs.org/gromacs/ ].
    # NOTE for the PI: 2025.4 and 2026.x also exist upstream by now; 2025.3 is a
    # deliberate pin, not "the newest". Bump only with intent.
    GROMACS_VERSION=2025.3

    # Official URL, copied verbatim from the GROMACS 2025.3 download page:
    GROMACS_URL="https://ftp.gromacs.org/gromacs/gromacs-${GROMACS_VERSION}.tar.gz"

    # INTEGRITY: GROMACS publishes only an MD5 for the source tarball — there is
    # no sha256 on the download page and no .sha256/SHA256SUMS file in the ftp
    # index. So BOTH of these are checked below:
    #
    #   (1) MD5 — OFFICIAL, straight off the GROMACS download page
    #       "https://ftp.gromacs.org/gromacs/gromacs-2025.3.tar.gz
    #        (md5sum 5a2315b6f6e13b091bbbbfddee9eb62b)"
    #       [verified: https://manual.gromacs.org/documentation/2025.3/download.html]
    GROMACS_MD5=5a2315b6f6e13b091bbbbfddee9eb62b
    #
    #   (2) SHA256 — NOT published by GROMACS. The value below is independently
    #       corroborated by TWO unrelated packaging projects that each hash the
    #       same official tarball, and they agree byte-for-byte:
    #         * Spack:     version("2025.3", sha256="8bdfca02...")
    #           https://github.com/spack/spack-packages/blob/develop/repos/spack_repo/builtin/packages/gromacs/package.py
    #         * EasyBuild: checksums = [{'gromacs-2025.3.tar.gz': '8bdfca02...'}]
    #           https://github.com/easybuilders/easybuild-easyconfigs/blob/develop/easybuild/easyconfigs/g/GROMACS/GROMACS-2025.3-foss-2025b-CUDA-12.9.1.eb
    #       Treat it as strongly corroborated, NOT as vendor-published. If it
    #       ever mismatches at build time, trust the official MD5 first and
    #       re-derive this value; do not silently delete the check.
    GROMACS_SHA256=8bdfca0268f3f10a7ca3c06e59b62f73ea02420c67211c0ff3912f32d7833c65

    # --- CMake pin (MUST be >= 3.28; jammy's apt cmake is 3.22.1) ------------
    # 4.0.3 is not a guess: EasyBuild's GROMACS-2025.3-foss-2025b-CUDA-12.9.1.eb
    # builds this exact GROMACS with ('CMake', '4.0.3') and CUDA 12.9.1, so the
    # GROMACS-2025.3 + CMake-4.0.3 + CUDA-12.9.1 triple is known-good upstream
    #   [verified: the easyconfig linked above].
    CMAKE_VERSION=4.0.3
    # sha256 IS officially published by Kitware, in the per-release checksum
    # asset cmake-4.0.3-SHA-256.txt:
    #   585ae9e0...  cmake-4.0.3-linux-x86_64.tar.gz
    #   [verified: https://github.com/Kitware/CMake/releases/download/v4.0.3/cmake-4.0.3-SHA-256.txt]
    CMAKE_SHA256=585ae9e013107bc8e7c7c9ce872cbdcbdff569e675b07ef57aacfb88c886faac

    # --- Build toolchain -----------------------------------------------------
    # NOTE: `cmake` is deliberately ABSENT from this list — apt would give 3.22.1,
    # below GROMACS 2025.3's hard 3.28 floor. Installed from upstream below.
    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        build-essential \
        ca-certificates \
        wget \
        libfftw3-dev \
        libopenblas-dev \
        pkg-config \
        python3
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

    # --- CMake >= 3.28 from upstream binary tarball --------------------------
    cd /opt
    wget -q "https://github.com/Kitware/CMake/releases/download/v${CMAKE_VERSION}/cmake-${CMAKE_VERSION}-linux-x86_64.tar.gz"
    echo "${CMAKE_SHA256}  cmake-${CMAKE_VERSION}-linux-x86_64.tar.gz" | sha256sum -c -
    tar xf "cmake-${CMAKE_VERSION}-linux-x86_64.tar.gz"
    mv "cmake-${CMAKE_VERSION}-linux-x86_64" /opt/cmake
    rm -f "cmake-${CMAKE_VERSION}-linux-x86_64.tar.gz"
    export PATH=/opt/cmake/bin:$PATH
    cmake --version   # must print >= 3.28

    # --- Fetch + verify GROMACS source --------------------------------------
    cd /opt
    wget -q "${GROMACS_URL}"
    # Integrity check (MDDB/provenance discipline: pin + hash every input).
    # Both checks are ACTIVE. The MD5 is the vendor-published one; the SHA256 is
    # the cross-corroborated one (see provenance notes above).
    echo "${GROMACS_MD5}  gromacs-${GROMACS_VERSION}.tar.gz" | md5sum -c -
    echo "${GROMACS_SHA256}  gromacs-${GROMACS_VERSION}.tar.gz" | sha256sum -c -
    tar xf "gromacs-${GROMACS_VERSION}.tar.gz"
    cd "gromacs-${GROMACS_VERSION}"
    mkdir build && cd build

    # --- Configure -----------------------------------------------------------
    # All flag spellings below are checked against the GROMACS 2025.3 install
    # guide [verified: https://manual.gromacs.org/documentation/2025.3/install-guide/index.html]:
    #
    # GMX_GPU=CUDA            : NVIDIA CUDA acceleration on. Guide, verbatim:
    #                           "-DGMX_GPU=CUDA to build with NVIDIA CUDA support
    #                           enabled." Requirements it states: "CUDA toolkit
    #                           version 12.1 or newer" and "GPU with compute
    #                           capability 5.0 or higher" — 12.9.1 / cc 7.0+9.0
    #                           both satisfy this. (Caveat: GROMACS' own CI tests
    #                           CUDA 12.1, 12.5.1 and 12.6, so 12.9 is above the
    #                           CI-tested set while still above the documented
    #                           minimum; EasyBuild ships a 2025.3 + CUDA 12.9.1
    #                           easyconfig, which is the corroboration for it.)
    # GMX_MPI=OFF             : no cross-node MPI; thread-MPI (intra-node) is
    #                           ON by default ("GROMACS can run in parallel on
    #                           multiple cores of a single workstation using its
    #                           built-in thread-MPI. No user action is required")
    #                           and is all a 1-GPU / single-node smoke test
    #                           needs. Both clusters are SINGLE-NODE Slurm
    #                           [live: report 07], so multi-node MPI buys
    #                           nothing here; add it later if scaling out.
    # GMX_CUDA_TARGET_SM      : real-arch (SASS) codegen for the two GPUs we own.
    #                           "70"=V100 Volta, "90"=H100 Hopper. Guide:
    #                           GMX_CUDA_TARGET_SM / GMX_CUDA_TARGET_COMPUTE
    #                           "take a semicolon delimited string with the two
    #                           digit suffixes of CUDA (virtual) architectures
    #                           names, for instance '60;75;86'" — so "70;90" is
    #                           the right shape. Both sm_70 and sm_90 are in
    #                           nvcc 12.9.1's supported SM list (URL above).
    #                           (Known 2025.3 CMake quirk: gmxManageNvccConfig
    #                           strips letter suffixes from CCs, breaking values
    #                           like "120f" — see EasyBuild's
    #                           GROMACS-2025.3_retain_cuda_cc_suffix.patch. Our
    #                           plain "70;90" has no suffix, so it is unaffected.)
    # GMX_CUDA_TARGET_COMPUTE : intended to embed PTX for 90 only, so future/
    #                           newer GPUs can JIT while the binary stays lean.
    #                           CORRECTED 2026-07-30: this is NOT what the
    #                           built image actually contains. `cuobjdump -lptx`
    #                           on the real binary shows PTX embedded for BOTH
    #                           70 and 90 (SM_ELF=sm_70;sm_90,
    #                           SM_PTX=sm_70;sm_90, 98 ELF + 98 PTX records) —
    #                           GROMACS' CMake evidently emits PTX for every SM
    #                           target regardless of GMX_CUDA_TARGET_COMPUTE.
    #                           This is functionally harmless (more PTX = more
    #                           forward-portable, a marginally larger binary)
    #                           and is a documentation correction only — the
    #                           flag itself is UNCHANGED.
    #                           [observed 2026-07-29 via cuobjdump -lptx, sass_portability_audit leaf]
    # GMX_DOUBLE=OFF          : single precision — the standard, GPU-supported build.
    # GMX_BUILD_OWN_FFTW=OFF  : use the distro libfftw3-dev installed above.
    #                           HONEST CAVEAT: upstream recommends the opposite —
    #                           "the GROMACS team recommends either that you
    #                           permit the GROMACS installation to download and
    #                           build FFTW from source automatically for you (use
    #                           cmake -DGMX_BUILD_OWN_FFTW=ON)", because distro
    #                           FFTW packages are typically double-precision-first
    #                           and may miss AVX/AVX2 codelets. OFF is kept here
    #                           for a hermetic, download-free configure step; it
    #                           is a PERFORMANCE trade, not a correctness one.
    #                           Flip to ON if PME-on-CPU throughput matters.
    # CUDA_TOOLKIT_ROOT_DIR   : guide shows exactly
    #                           `cmake .. -DGMX_GPU=CUDA -DCUDA_TOOLKIT_ROOT_DIR=/usr/local/cuda`.
    cmake .. \
        -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
        -DGMX_GPU=CUDA \
        -DGMX_MPI=OFF \
        -DGMX_CUDA_TARGET_SM="70;90" \
        -DGMX_CUDA_TARGET_COMPUTE="90" \
        -DGMX_DOUBLE=OFF \
        -DGMX_BUILD_OWN_FFTW=OFF \
        -DGMX_SIMD=AVX2_256 \
        -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/opt/gromacs \
        -DCUDA_TOOLKIT_ROOT_DIR=/usr/local/cuda
    # [verified] GMX_SIMD=AVX2_256 — RESOLVED cycle-006. Was the file's last
    # `[assumption]`; the value is UNCHANGED but is now a documented decision
    # rather than a conservative guess. The resolution is counter-intuitive
    # (both CPUs support AVX-512 and we still keep AVX2_256), so read the whole
    # note before "fixing" it.
    #
    # STEP 1 — THE HARDWARE, finally recorded (report 07 had no `lscpu`):
    #   * banyan: Intel Xeon Gold 6530  (Emerald Rapids)
    #   * dgx1:   Intel Xeon Gold 6130  (Skylake-SP)
    #   BOTH carry the AVX-512 core set: avx512f avx512dq avx512bw avx512vl
    #   avx512cd. banyan additionally has VNNI/BF16/FP16/IFMA/VBMI + AMX; dgx1
    #   has ONLY the core set, so dgx1 is the least common denominator.
    #     [source: __reports__/p53-mdm2/10-cluster_state_refresh_v0.md §4
    #              (lscpu on both clusters, cycle-006, commit c8f2cc2)]
    #
    # STEP 2 — `AVX_512` IS a valid GMX_SIMD value for 2025.3. The accepted list
    #   is: None, SSE2, SSE4.1, AVX_128_FMA, AVX_256, AVX2_128, AVX2_256,
    #   AVX_512, AVX_512_KNL, IBM_VSX, ARM_NEON_ASIMD, ARM_SVE (+ Reference for
    #   developers). So the naive least-common-denominator rule would say AVX_512.
    #     [verified: https://manual.gromacs.org/documentation/2025.3/install-guide/index.html]
    #
    # STEP 3 — BUT THE GROMACS DOCS OVERRIDE THE NAIVE RULE. The 2025.3 install
    #   guide's AVX_512 entry says, VERBATIM:
    #     "With GPU accelerated runs `AVX2_256` can also be faster on high-end
    #      Skylake CPUs with both 512-bit FMA units enabled."
    #     [verified: install guide URL above, GMX_SIMD section]
    #   Our runs ARE GPU-accelerated by construction (-DGMX_GPU=CUDA above;
    #   `gmx mdrun -nb gpu` in smoke_submit.sbatch), and dgx1 IS a high-end
    #   Skylake (Skylake-SP Xeon Gold). That sentence describes this exact
    #   machine and this exact workload — it is not a generic aside.
    #
    # STEP 4 — THE CONCLUSION IS ROBUST TO THE ONE UNCHECKED FACT. The guide also
    #   notes AVX_512 is fastest "on the higher-end desktop and server
    #   processors", while models such as "Xeon Bronze and Silver" have "only one
    #   AVX512 FMA unit and therefore on these processors `AVX2_256` is faster"
    #   [verified: same section]. We did NOT verify the Gold 6130's FMA-unit
    #   count — and do not need to, because both branches agree:
    #     * one FMA unit  -> guide says AVX2_256 IS faster
    #     * two FMA units -> the GPU-accelerated sentence applies, AVX2_256 CAN
    #                        be faster
    #   No branch makes AVX_512 the documented choice for a GPU-accelerated run
    #   on this CPU.
    #
    # SAFETY: AVX2_256 is "Present on Intel Haswell (and later) processors (2013)
    #   and AMD Zen3 and later (2020)" [verified: same section]. Both CPUs are far
    #   newer than Haswell, so there is no illegal-instruction risk on either
    #   cluster. One .sif, runs on both.
    #
    # WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM: no benchmark was run, on either CPU. banyan's
    #   Emerald Rapids may well be faster with AVX_512
    #     [assumption: the 2025.3 guide's AVX_512 caveats are written about
    #      Skylake-X/SP and Zen4 and say nothing about Emerald Rapids either way;
    #      that later Intel generations narrowed the AVX-512 downside is general
    #      industry lore, NOT verified against a citable source here.]
    #   If CPU-side PME ever becomes the bottleneck ON BANYAN SPECIFICALLY, the
    #   escape hatch is a PER-ARCHITECTURE build (-DGMX_BINARY_SUFFIX, the guide's
    #   own advice for heterogeneous fleets), NOT raising this flag on the shared
    #   image. The single-.sif-run-anywhere constraint is what pins this value.
    #
    # DO NOT delete the flag and rely on CMake auto-detection: the guide says it
    # detects "the SIMD instruction set supported by the CPU architecture (on
    # which the configuring is done)" — for one .sif shared across two CPU
    # generations via one NFS home, that pins the image to the BUILD host.

    # --- Build + install -----------------------------------------------------
    make -j"$(nproc)"
    # make check   # optional: GROMACS regression tests (slow; needs a GPU at
    #              # build time for the GPU tests — skip in an unattended build).
    make install

    # Clean the source tree to keep the image small.
    cd /opt && rm -rf "gromacs-${GROMACS_VERSION}" "gromacs-${GROMACS_VERSION}.tar.gz"

%environment
    # Put gmx on PATH for `singularity exec ... gmx ...` invocations.
    export PATH=/opt/gromacs/bin:$PATH
    # GROMACS also ships GMXRC; source it if a downstream tool needs the full env.
    # . /opt/gromacs/bin/GMXRC  (kept commented: PATH above is enough for `gmx`)

%runscript
    # Default action when the .sif is "run" directly: prove the build is sane.
    echo "=== GROMACS container (p53-mdm2 / usd-bio) ==="
    exec /opt/gromacs/bin/gmx --version

%test
    # Runs at the END of `singularity build` (build-time sanity, CPU-only —
    # no GPU is present in a build sandbox, so this only proves the binary links
    # and reports its build config, NOT that GPU execution works. Real GPU
    # verification happens in the PI-gated smoke_submit.sbatch on a GPU node).
    #
    # This gate has the SAME exposure `./Dockerfile`'s equivalent RUN documents
    # in full: a build sandbox has no NVIDIA driver, so `gmx --version` could in
    # principle abort on a missing libcuda.so.1. RESOLVED 2026-07-30: it does
    # not — the 2025.3 gmx binary's NEEDED list has no libcuda.so.1 entry
    # (`readelf -d`: libgromacs.so.10, libstdc++.so.6, libgcc_s.so.1, libc.so.6
    # only), and the 2026-07-29 Docker-twin build reached exit 0 against this
    # exact gate with `CUDA driver: 0.0`. See ./Dockerfile's header for the full
    # evidence; this is a shared risk closed by the same observation.
    set -e
    /opt/gromacs/bin/gmx --version
    # Confirm the binary was actually compiled with GPU support:
    /opt/gromacs/bin/gmx --version | grep -i "GPU support" || \
        { echo "FAIL: gmx reports no GPU support"; exit 1; }

%help
    GPU-enabled GROMACS 2025.3 (CUDA 12.9.1) for the usd-bio p53-MDM2 self-run MD
    track. Built to run on BOTH banyan (H100/sm_90) and dgx1 (V100/sm_70) via
    Singularity `--nv`. This exact .def has never been built directly (Route A
    failed, observed — see below); its Docker twin ./Dockerfile HAS been built
    (2026-07-29, Slurm job 30, exit 0) — see ./README.md for the ordered,
    PI-gated build/stage/smoke-test steps and their current status.

    All pinned versions/hashes carry an inline source URL in this file, and NO
    value is left as an assumption — GMX_SIMD (the last one) resolved to
    AVX2_256 in cycle-006 against the GROMACS 2025.3 install guide, once the
    cluster CPU models were recorded. See the [verified] note by that flag.

    BUILD ROUTE: use Route B (docker build on banyan -> docker save ->
    singularity build docker-archive://...) — this succeeded on 2026-07-29.
    Native `singularity build` from this .def FAILS, observed, not merely
    expected — no /etc/subuid mapping for this user on either cluster, so
    --fakeroot is unavailable. See the header for the full reasoning
    and the one command that would disprove it.

    Typical use once a gromacs.sif exists on the shared home:
        singularity exec --nv \
            -B /home/eliott/p53mdm2/in:/data/in \
            -B /home/eliott/p53mdm2/out:/data/out \
            gromacs.sif gmx mdrun -deffnm /data/out/run
